WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.320
Hi.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.320
Hello, everyone.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.440
Welcome to my talk.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:13.960
I'll be presenting to you today ggsql, which is a tool in alpha right now.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:20.280
The idea is basically that we have the grammar of graphics for SQL.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.920
To explain this a little bit, the grammar of graphics is a theory, and we combine it

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.000
with SQL.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:36.439
You have a grammar and a language, so it can kind of go together.

00:00:36.439 --> 00:00:43.919
I want to make a quick example to get everyone's noses pointing the same way.

00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:45.599
You may all recognize this.

00:00:45.599 --> 00:00:47.840
You have a SQL query.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.400
Anybody coming from the R ecosystem might recognize these penguins.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.080
But the problem, if you're working with data like this, is that it's not immediately obvious

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.360
what's going on here.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.400
What is going on with these penguins?

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:10.199
The thing that we are adding is that if you work from WB, you can install the ggsql extension

00:01:10.199 --> 00:01:18.000
from the community extensions, and then you can add additional syntax to make a plot out

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.519
of this.

00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:21.800
This is your query executed.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.919
It has only penguins living on the Biscay Island, and we are visualizing the build length

00:01:27.919 --> 00:01:34.120
of their beaks versus their build depth, and we're drawing these with a point layer, and

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.800
this has all been done before.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.080
But now we can also draw a trend line, make some groups, and you can all do this from

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.360
the comfort of your SQL analysis.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.440
I was explicitly told no demos today.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.279
That's why I'm trying to get you to do the demo.

00:01:54.279 --> 00:02:01.120
There's the ggsql.org website, and you see the text input there.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.239
It's an interactive example.

00:02:02.239 --> 00:02:07.680
You can try it out right now if you want.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:14.839
And now I'm going to brag a little bit, because I have been working as a maintainer of ggplot2

00:02:14.839 --> 00:02:22.160
and wrote my first SQL query maybe seven months ago, but we have had a long time of

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.919
maintaining ggplot2.

00:02:23.919 --> 00:02:34.580
It's the most downloaded R package at all, so even more popular than some hardcore infrastructure

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:46.300
packages like rcpp or jsonlite, but the ggplot2 project is mature, but also constrained by

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:52.779
backward compatibility, and for us, ggplot2 is a new adventure with a carte blanche for

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:56.419
new ideas.

00:02:56.419 --> 00:03:04.839
And for those not on the same page yet, the grammar of graphics is a theory that was developed

00:03:04.839 --> 00:03:11.199
by Leland Wilkinson, which was published in this book.

00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:18.660
And this theory has several software implementations, of which ggplot2 is one, but there are also

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:29.419
ggplot9 for mPython, and notably missing from this list is SQL.

00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:33.100
So what do we kind of mean?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:40.300
We presume that many of your data analytics workflows are kind of like this.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:46.899
You have your data living somewhere in a database, preferably duckDB for this audience, and you

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:54.860
send your data either to R or Python, use pandas or dplyr to do your data wrangling,

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:59.380
and then you make a plot with ggplot9 or ggplot2.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:05.020
The big downside of this is that you need to cross an ecosystem gap, so you go from

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:11.820
your SQL, from your duckDB to R or Python, and this is inconvenient.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:21.899
So we try to posture ggSQL as belonging right here at the SQL side of things, so you can

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:31.820
just not interrupt your workflows and get plots straight out of your databases.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.820
We are not the first to attempt this particular strategy.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.220
This path has been trodden by others before.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:45.660
For example, there is a mini-plot extension in the community extensions as well.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:51.019
Mini-plot has a functional approach, so you have this scatter chart function, which you

00:04:51.019 --> 00:04:57.100
give a list of your variables, and that will also produce the same plot as the first one

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.100
I showed you.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.859
However, you can only make a scatter plot with this function, and it doesn't have any

00:05:01.859 --> 00:05:08.660
other customisations for it, so this is quite constrained, and the opposite effect is if

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.779
you want to build everything in a function, you get a giant monolith which is also terrible

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:15.220
to use.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.540
So that's why we're not doing a functional approach.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.420
What we're also not doing is treading into Microsoft territory and make business intelligence

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:35.079
tools, so there's this closed-source Microsoft BI Power BI desktop which allows you to also

00:05:35.079 --> 00:05:40.000
make plots from your database-backed data.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.519
This is point-and-clicking, and this is not open source, and we believe that code is the

00:05:45.519 --> 00:05:50.920
primary way in which visualisation should be expressed.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:56.200
So we believe code is better than point-and-clicking.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:03.799
And actually, when you think about it a little bit, like language, grammar, there are a bunch

00:06:03.799 --> 00:06:06.640
of commonalities that the two have.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.640
Both are declarative.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:14.720
SQL, for example, allows you to speak into information structures, like give me the revenue

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:21.000
grouped by month, whereas the grammar of graphics allows you to speak information and codings,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.079
like show revenue as bars by month.

00:06:25.079 --> 00:06:28.959
Both of them are compositional with a small core grammar, and in SQL you have to select

00:06:28.959 --> 00:06:34.200
clauses, grouped by where, whereas in the grammar of graphics, you have things like

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:41.600
layers or things like scales, and you can recombine all these different words to get

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.839
the task done.

00:06:44.839 --> 00:06:50.480
SQL is also sort of portable.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.200
Now I'm not so sure any more after hearing about the Spice Park migration talk, but in

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:02.279
theory, SQL might be portable.

00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:13.079
So yeah, you can run SQL on DocDB, on other database backends, and similarly-ish, you

00:07:13.079 --> 00:07:19.720
can also execute the grammar of graphics on different backends, like ggplot2, the difference

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:27.279
is mostly that the syntax is not conserved across the execution platforms.

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:36.839
I am going to skim this one, because I just need to tell you that what we receive from

00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:42.200
the user is this select statement, for example, followed by visualize.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.119
What we do is we split the two.

00:07:45.119 --> 00:07:49.000
We throw the select to the database.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.920
We keep the visualize.

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:58.640
We build the plots, and out comes a graphic.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:05.320
So to demonstrate this a little bit more fully, it is a darling in the database community

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:10.720
to reproduce this famous chart by Charles Minard.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:20.559
This chart is an illustration of Napoleon's 1912 campaign into Russia, and the main things

00:08:20.559 --> 00:08:28.000
that we can see here, there's a beige line going to the right, and there's a black line

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.000
returning.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:36.440
The thickness of the line indicates how many troops Napoleon's army has left.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:44.479
So as you can see it well, but as Napoleon marches to Moscow, he's losing troops and

00:08:44.479 --> 00:08:45.479
troops and troops.

00:08:45.479 --> 00:08:50.039
He reaches Moscow and then has to retreat and loses troops and troops and troops.

00:08:50.039 --> 00:08:57.000
So while technically he did get to Moscow, he didn't come back very well.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:05.760
So that is what this chart shows, and we can have very similar data.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:12.640
We can have the longitude and latitude coordinates of where the troops are at some point, the

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:18.080
number of troops that Napoleon had at that point, whether they're advancing or retreating,

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.039
and which group of the army it is, and the simplest thing that you can do is just add

00:09:23.039 --> 00:09:27.679
a visualize, longitude is X, latitude is Y, draw a path.

00:09:27.679 --> 00:09:34.159
So you have spoken this plot into existence, and then you can from this basis you can start

00:09:34.159 --> 00:09:38.719
iterating and building more and more stuff up on top the plot.

00:09:38.719 --> 00:09:43.599
For example, you can properly split the lines by doing a partition.

00:09:43.599 --> 00:09:47.640
You can add the direction as a color.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.359
You can also add the line width representing the number of survivors.

00:09:52.359 --> 00:09:55.719
You can add a scale to have the colors match more.

00:09:55.719 --> 00:09:57.960
You can have a scale to have thicker lines.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:07.200
You can add a text layer to give some context of which cities are at what place.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:14.799
And lastly, you can use the spatial extension as well to do some map projections.

00:10:14.799 --> 00:10:24.880
For example, if we project to Mercator, then we will have a properly proportioned plot.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:36.359
We can also add a spatial layer, this was added Monday, where you can just have geospatial

00:10:36.359 --> 00:10:46.400
data and you can flip through a few projections, zoom in, and as a finishing touch, put the

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.479
correct labels at the right spot.

00:10:48.479 --> 00:10:51.320
So this is what you could do.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.880
And our idea is that we want it to work wherever.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.119
We distribute it as a lone executable.

00:10:58.119 --> 00:11:07.760
We don't want to bundle it with R and Python environments, so we don't have a runtime.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.200
We want to constrain the execution so that the possibility to execute malicious code

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:13.200
is minimal.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.719
We have a variety of integrations with, for example, a docDB extension, we have a command

00:11:17.719 --> 00:11:20.960
line interface, a Jupyter kernel.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:25.239
And we also hope that it is kind of nice to work with assistance for this.

00:11:25.239 --> 00:11:29.520
Of course, there's a bunch of SQL training data out there that's not out there for GGSQL,

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:35.919
but we hope that some properties of SQL will transfer well to GGSQL.

00:11:35.919 --> 00:11:41.799
We are also kind of exploring for how do you want to maybe you want to use LLM assistance

00:11:41.799 --> 00:11:46.799
for interfacing with your dashboards and your data visualizations.

00:11:46.799 --> 00:11:52.440
There's a query chat package out there that can help you with this, and we wrote a skill

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:57.880
to also teach your LLM assistance to work with GGSQL.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:03.479
You can hopefully get this someday, wherever SQL is, but for now, there's interactive examples

00:12:03.479 --> 00:12:04.479
on the website.

00:12:04.479 --> 00:12:08.280
There's language support for VS Code and Positron.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.479
There's a kernel for Jupyter and Quarto.

00:12:10.479 --> 00:12:15.479
There's a command line interface, R, Python, and the WebAssembly, which drives the interactive

00:12:15.479 --> 00:12:16.679
examples.

00:12:16.679 --> 00:12:21.119
And of course, the DougDB extension is actually a DougDB sandwich, really.

00:12:21.119 --> 00:12:28.039
It's DougDB in the back, GGSQL in the middle, and DougDB on front.

00:12:28.039 --> 00:12:29.039
So yeah.

00:12:29.039 --> 00:12:33.880
In closing, we have made a new SQL-based tool for data visualization based on the grammar

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.880
of graphics.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:43.000
DougDB is a first-class citizen herein, and with that, I would like to thank you, and

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:48.960
in particular Posit, for paying me money, and Thomas, George, and Hadley, who collaborated

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:49.960
with me on this.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:49.960


